import mundane theorem    stdlib/sums/sum-termwise
import mundane theorem    stdlib/sums/sum-nonneg
import mundane theorem    stdlib/sums/sum-zero-terms
import mundane theorem    stdlib/sums/sum-constant
import mundane theorem    stdlib/numbers/square-nonneg
import mundane theorem    stdlib/numbers/square-zero
import mundane theorem    stdlib/numbers/multiply-le
import mundane axiom      stdlib/numbers/trichotomy

theorem cauchy-schwarz
  metamath    csbren
  note        The sum of the squares (a(k)·t + b(k))² is never negative, and it
              is A·t² + 2B·t + C. If A = 0, every a(k) is 0 and both sides are
              0. If A > 0, t = −B/A gives C − B²/A ≥ 0, so B² ≤ A·C.
  let n ∈ ℕ                                                           (H1)
  let a : {1, …, n} → ℝ                                               (H2)
  let b : {1, …, n} → ℝ                                               (H3)
  then (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k))² ≤ (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²)·(Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)²)

1.  For all t ∈ ℝ, t²·(Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²) + 2t·(Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k)) + Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)² ≥ 0.
    proof
    let t ∈ ℝ                                                         (K1)

    1.1.  For all k ∈ {1, …, n}, (a(k)·t + b(k))² ≥ 0.
          mun:square-nonneg x := a(k)·t + b(k)
          requires a(k)·t + b(k) ∈ ℝ: membership, from H2, H3, K1

    1.2.  Σ(k = 1 to n) (a(k)·t + b(k))² ≥ 0
          mun:sum-nonneg a := 1, b := n, from 1.1

    1.3.  Σ(k = 1 to n) (a(k)·t + b(k))² = t²·(Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²) + 2t·(Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k)) + Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)²
          algebra

    1.4.  t²·(Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²) + 2t·(Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k)) + Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)² ≥ 0
          substitute Σ(k = 1 to n) (a(k)·t + b(k))² = t²·(Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²) + 2t·(Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k)) + Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)² (line 1.3) into line 1.2

2.  For all k ∈ {1, …, n}, a(k)² ≥ 0.
    mun:square-nonneg x := a(k), from H2

3.  Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)² ≥ 0
    mun:sum-nonneg a := 1, b := n, from 2

4.  Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)² < 0 or Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)² = 0 or 0 < Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²
    mun:trichotomy x := Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²

5.  (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k))² ≤ (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²)·(Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)²)
    cases, from 4

    case
    note a sum of squares is never negative, so this case cannot occur
    assume Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)² < 0                                    (C1)

    5.1.  not Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)² < 0
          inequalities, from 3, contradicting C1

    case
    note every a(k) is 0, so both sides are 0
    assume Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)² = 0                                    (C2)

    5.2.  For all k ∈ {1, …, n}, a(k)² = 0.
          mun:sum-zero-terms a := 1, b := n, from C2, 2

    5.3.  For all k ∈ {1, …, n}, a(k)·b(k) = 0.
          proof
          let k ∈ {1, …, n}                                           (K4)

          5.3.1.  a(k)² = 0
                  instantiate k := k in line 5.2, from K4

          5.3.2.  a(k) = 0
                  mun:square-zero x := a(k), from H2, K4, 5.3.1

          5.3.3.  a(k)·b(k) = 0
                  algebra, from 5.3.2
                  requires a(k) ∈ ℝ: from H2, K4
                  requires b(k) ∈ ℝ: from H3, K4

    5.4.  Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k) = Σ(k = 1 to n) 0
          mun:sum-termwise a := 1, b := n, from 5.3

    5.5.  Σ(k = 1 to n) 0 = (n − 1 + 1)·0
          mun:sum-constant a := 1, b := n, c := 0
          requires 1 ≤ n: inequalities, from H1

    5.6.  (n − 1 + 1)·0 = 0
          algebra

    5.7.  Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k) = 0
          calculation
            Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k) = Σ(k = 1 to n) 0      5.4
                                    = (n − 1 + 1)·0        5.5
                                    = 0                    5.6

    5.8.  (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k))² = 0
          algebra, from 5.7

    5.9.  (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²)·(Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)²) = 0
          algebra, from C2

    5.10. (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k))² = (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²)·(Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)²)
          transitivity, from 5.8, 5.9

    5.11. (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k))² ≤ (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²)·(Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)²)
          inequalities
            (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k))² = (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²)·(Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)²)    5.10
            (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k))² ≤ (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²)·(Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)²)    weakening

    case
    note step 1 at t = −B/A, where the quadratic is smallest, gives C − B²/A ≥ 0
    assume 0 < Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²                                    (C3)

    let A = Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)², a real number                        (D1)
           membership
           reads the sum of the squares of the a's

    let B = Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k), a real number                    (D2)
           membership
           reads the sum of the products

    let C = Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)², a real number                        (D3)
           membership
           reads the sum of the squares of the b's

    5.12. A > 0
          inequalities, from C3, D1

    5.13. (−B/A)²·A + 2(−B/A)·B + C ≥ 0
          instantiate t := −B/A in line 1, from D1, D2, D3
          requires A > 0: inequalities, from C3, D1

    5.14. (−B/A)²·A + 2(−B/A)·B + C = C − B²/A
          algebra
          requires A ≠ 0: weakening, from 5.12

    5.15. C − B²/A ≥ 0
          transitivity, from 5.13, 5.14
          requires A ≠ 0: weakening, from 5.12

    5.16. B²/A ≤ C
          inequalities
            C − B²/A ≥ 0         5.15
            C ≥ B²/A             adding B²/A
          requires A ≠ 0: weakening, from 5.12

    5.17. A·(B²/A) ≤ A·C
          mun:multiply-le, from 5.16
          requires A > 0: inequalities, from C3, D1

    5.18. A·(B²/A) = B²
          algebra
          requires A ≠ 0: weakening, from 5.12

    5.19. B² ≤ A·C
          substitute A·(B²/A) = B² (line 5.18) into line 5.17

    5.20. (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k))² ≤ (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²)·(Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)²)
          calculation
            (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)·b(k))² = B²      D2
                                       ≤ A·C     5.19
                                       = (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²)·C    D1
                                       = (Σ(k = 1 to n) a(k)²)·(Σ(k = 1 to n) b(k)²)    D3
